":"Annadanam (free meals) served 11am–1pm daily.","accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Cochin International Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":45,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Vaikam Road railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":8.7,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Local/regional tradition","architecture_style":null,"construction_century":null},{"id":"f8957e95-13cd-43ca-995a-822a243b96ea","name":"Srikalahasteeswara temple","deity":"Shiva as Kalahasteeswara (Vayu)","sampradaya":"Shaiva","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Andhra Pradesh","district":"Tirupati","town":"Putturu","latitude":13.74944,"longitude":79.69833,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Swarnamukhi River","river_distance_km":0.2,"best_season_notes":null,"submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-11-04T11:04:54.58588+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-05T02:38:03.965515+00:00","sthala_purana":"A temple at Srikalahasti, about 36 km from Tirupati, where Shiva is worshipped as the wind aspect in the Vayu Lingam, one of the Pancha Bhuta Sthalams. It is also regarded as a Rahu-Ketu kshetra and is tied by tradition to the devotee Kannappa.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":null,"morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Tirupati Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":21.3,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Sri Kalahasti railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":1.7,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Wikipedia (Srikalahasteeswara temple), summarised; location via OpenStreetMap","architecture_style":null,"construction_century":null},{"id":"f8c3a762-141d-4b29-9d6c-1f23ba476adf","name":"Chausath Yogini Temple, Hirapur","deity":"The 64 Yoginis (Shakta-Tantric tradition), with a central Mahamaya/Kali image — 56 of the original 64 stone idols survive","sampradaya":"Shakta","significance":["Tantric shrine","Hypaethral (roofless) temple"],"country":"India","state":"Odisha","district":"Khurda","town":"Hirapur","latitude":20.22651,"longitude":85.8756,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Dayaa River","river_distance_km":2.4,"best_season_notes":"Kali Puja is the principal festival here; worship at this Tantric site is non-mainstream compared to typical temple puja.","submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-09-22T08:41:30.569832+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-05T02:38:03.965515+00:00","sthala_purana":"Built around 864 CE and attributed to Hiradevi, a queen of the Bhauma-Kara dynasty and mother of King Subhakaradeva II, this is considered by most scholarly accounts to be India's earliest known Chausath Yogini temple — though the precise 9th-century dating rests on limited epigraphic and traditional evidence rather than firm inscription. It is a rare circular, roofless (hypaethral) shrine only about 7.6m across, reflecting Tantric ritual practice that calls for open-sky worship invoking the five elements, with 64 individual yogini niches set into the circular stone wall. In the 16th century, the shrine's idols were reportedly damaged in a raid attributed to the general Kalapahad — a traditional attribution, treated here with appropriate caution rather than as settled fact.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":null,"morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Biju Patnaik International Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":7.1,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Bhubaneswar railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":5.6,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Bhauma-Kara dynasty tradition; art-historical scholarship","architecture_style":"Rare circular hypaethral (roofless) shrine, sandstone construction","construction_century":9},{"id":"f9217f5b-f8ce-42ad-859e-a9d50466c36b","name":"Kazheesirama Vinnagaram","deity":"Vishnu as Trivikrama","sampradaya":"Vaishnava","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Tamil Nadu","district":"Mayiladuthurai","town":"Kovilpattu","latitude":11.24056,"longitude":79.73139,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Uppanar River","river_distance_km":3.7,"best_season_notes":null,"submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-11-04T11:04:38.937899+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-05T02:38:03.965515+00:00","sthala_purana":"A Divya Desam in Sirkazhi, also called Thadalan Kovil, with Lakshmi as Loganayagi. Concentric walls and a three-tiered rajagopuram enclose the shrines, including those of Lakshmi and Andal. The Chakra Tirtham tank lies behind it, and the Vaikasi Brahmotsavam is the main festival. It follows the Tenkalai mode of worship.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":null,"morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Neyveli Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":47,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Sirkazhi railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":0.4,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Wikipedia (Kazheesirama Vinnagaram), summarised; location via OpenStreetMap","architecture_style":"Dravidian","con